"[2604.00223v1] Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00223v1)\nciteturn36210view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00223v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 31 Mar 2026]\nL9: # Title:Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Hoang-Chau Luong , cite6†Dat Ba Tran , cite7†Lingwei Chen L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation, by Hoang-Chau Luong and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Reverse Kullback-Leibler (RKL) divergence has recently emerged as the preferred objective for large language model (LLM) distillation, consistently outperforming forward KL (FKL), particularly in regimes with large vocabularies and significant teacher-student capacity mismatch, where RKL focuses learning on dominant modes rather than enforcing dense alignment. However, RKL introduces a structural limitation that drives the student toward overconfident predictions.\nL17: We first provide an analysis of RKL by decomposing its gradients into target and non-target components, and show that non-target gradients consistently push the target logit upward even when the student already matches the teacher, thereby reducing output diversity. In addition, RKL provides weak supervision over non-target classes, leading to poor tail alignment.\nL18: To address these issues, we propose Diversity-aware RKL (DRKL), which removes this gradient effect and strengthens non-target supervision while preserving the optimization benefits of RKL. Extensive experiments across datasets and model families demonstrate that DRKL consistently outperforms FKL, RKL, and other state-of-the-art distillation objectives, achieving better performance and a superior fidelity-diversity trade-off.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2604.00223 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2604.00223v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.00223†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Hoang-Chau Luong [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Tue, 31 Mar 2026 20:39:47 UTC (886 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation, by Hoang-Chau Luong and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite8†View PDF L35:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.LG\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2026-04 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: cite22†cs.AI L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer