3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 31 Mar 2026]\nL9: # Title:Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Lam M. Nguyen , cite6†Dzung T. Phan , cite7†Jayant Kalagnanam L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization, by Lam M. Nguyen and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Shuffling strategies for stochastic gradient descent (SGD), including incremental gradient, shuffle-once, and random reshuffling, are supported by rigorous convergence analyses for arbitrary within-epoch permutations. In particular, random reshuffling is known to improve optimization constants relative to cyclic and shuffle-once schemes.\nL17: However, existing theory offers limited guidance on how to design new data-ordering schemes that further improve optimization constants or stability beyond random reshuffling. In this paper, we design a pipeline using a large language model (LLM)-guided program evolution framework to discover an effective shuffling rule for without-replacement SGD. Abstracting from this instance, we identify two fundamental structural components: block reshuffling and paired reversal.\nL18: We analyze these components separately and show that block reshuffling strictly reduces prefix-gradient variance constants within the unified shuffling framework, yielding provable improvements over random reshuffling under mild conditions. Separately, we show that paired reversal symmetrizes the epoch map and cancels the leading order-dependent second-order term, reducing order sensitivity from quadratic to cubic in the step size.\nL19: Numerical experiments with the discovered algorithm validate the theory and demonstrate consistent gains over standard shuffling schemes across convex and nonconvex benchmarks.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2604.00260 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2604.00260v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.00260†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Lam Nguyen [cite13†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Tue, 31 Mar 2026 21:40:43 UTC (4,187 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization, by Lam M. Nguyen and 2 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite8†View PDF L36:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite14†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite15†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L45: \nL46: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2026-04 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite21†cs L51: cite22†math L52: cite23†math.OC L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite29†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL86: \nL87: [Input