45†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite46†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL157: \nL158: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite47†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL159: cite48†About†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2604.01025v1] Fast and Accurate Probing of In-Training LLMs' Downstream Performances (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01025v1)\nciteturn36210view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01025v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 1 Apr 2026]\nL9: # Title:Fast and Accurate Probing of In-Training LLMs' Downstream Performances\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Zhichen Liu , cite6†Tianle Lun , cite7†Zhibin Wen , cite8†Hao An , cite9†Yulin Ou , cite10†Jianhui Xu , cite11†Hao Zhang , cite12†Wenyi Fang , cite13†Yang Zheng , cite14†Yang Xu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Fast and Accurate Probing of In-Training LLMs' Downstream Performances, by Zhichen Liu and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:The paradigm of scaling Large Language Models (LLMs) in both parameter size and test time has pushed the boundaries of AI capabilities, but at the cost of making the traditional generative evaluation paradigm prohibitively expensive, therefore making the latency of LLM's in-training downstream performance evaluation unbearable.\nL17: However, simple metrics like training loss (perplexity) are not always correlated with downstream performance, as sometimes their trends diverge from the actual task outcomes. This dilemma calls for a method that is computationally efficient and sufficiently accurate in measuring model capabilities. To address this challenge, we introduce a new in-training evaluation paradigm that uses a lightweight probe for monitoring downstream performance.\nL18: The probes take the internal representations of LLM checkpoints (during training) as input and directly predict the checkpoint's performance on downstream tasks measured by success probability (i.e., pass@1). We design several probe architectures, validating their effectiveness using the OLMo3-7B's checkpoints across a diverse set of downstream tasks. The probes can accurately predict a checkpoint's performance (with avg.\nL19: AUROC$>$0.75), have decent generalizability across checkpoints (earlier predicts later), and reduce the computation latency from $\\sim$1 hr (using conventional generative evaluation method) to $\\sim$3 min. In sum, this work presents a practical and scalable in-training downstream evaluation paradigm, enabling a more agile, informed, and efficient LLM development process.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2604.01025 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2604.01025v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.01025†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Tianle Lun [cite20†view email ]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2604.00430v1] Secure Forgetting: A Framework for Privacy-